2026-10-26|Minimize Range After Removing a Subarray of Size K

Question:
Given an array arr of size n and an integer k, remove exactly one contiguous subarray of size k such that the difference between the maximum and minimum values in the remaining array is as small as possible. Return that minimum difference.

Approach:

1. If k equals n, removing the subarray leaves no elements, so the maximum and minimum of the remaining array are undefined. This case needs a special rule from the problem constraints.
2. Sort the array in non-decreasing order.
3. Consider every possible remaining window of size n - k in the sorted array.
4. For each window, calculate the difference between its largest and smallest elements.
5. Take the minimum difference across all windows.

Note: This approach assumes the removed elements can be chosen arbitrarily. If the problem strictly requires removing a contiguous subarray from the original, unsorted array, sorting changes which elements are adjacent and is not generally valid. For that interpretation, use prefix/suffix minimum and maximum arrays instead.

Solution:

class Solution {
public:
    int minDiff(int k, vector<int>& arr) {
        int n = arr.size();

        // Removing no elements leaves the original array.
        if (k == 0) {
            auto bounds = minmax_element(arr.begin(), arr.end());
            return *bounds.second - *bounds.first;
        }

        vector<int> prefixMin(n), prefixMax(n);
        vector<int> suffixMin(n), suffixMax(n);

        // Prefix minimum and maximum
        prefixMin[0] = prefixMax[0] = arr[0];

        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            prefixMin[i] = min(prefixMin[i - 1], arr[i]);
            prefixMax[i] = max(prefixMax[i - 1], arr[i]);
        }

        // Suffix minimum and maximum
        suffixMin[n - 1] = suffixMax[n - 1] = arr[n - 1];

        for (int i = n - 2; i >= 0; i--) {
            suffixMin[i] = min(suffixMin[i + 1], arr[i]);
            suffixMax[i] = max(suffixMax[i + 1], arr[i]);
        }

        int answer = INT_MAX;

        // Remove a subarray starting at i, of length k
        for (int i = 0; i + k <= n; i++) {
            int left = i - 1;
            int right = i + k;

            int mn = INT_MAX;
            int mx = INT_MIN;

            if (left >= 0) {
                mn = min(mn, prefixMin[left]);
                mx = max(mx, prefixMax[left]);
            }

            if (right < n) {
                mn = min(mn, suffixMin[right]);
                mx = max(mx, suffixMax[right]);
            }

            // If nothing remains, define the difference as 0.
            int diff = (mn == INT_MAX) ? 0 : mx - mn;

            answer = min(answer, diff);
        }

        return answer;
    }
};


Time Complexity: O(n log n), due to sorting.

Space Complexity: O(1) auxiliary space, excluding sorting implementation details.
